{
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "complaint_category",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "rationale": "Categorising the primary complaint gives a strong axis to group and prioritise pain points, directly serving 'what to concentrate on'. Samples map well: seat discomfort→seat_product_failure, reservation payment→billing_refund_dispute, flight swap→flight_delay_cancellation, seat reallocation→overbooking_seat_change. The positive review correctly yields Unknown/NULL. Well grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "staff_attitude_judgment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.75,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "rationale": "Partly overlaps with the original Staff Service rating, but the three-way positive/negative/mixed judgment derived from free text adds nuance beyond the numeric score. Samples are sound (pleasant crew→positive, friendly-but-limited employees→mixed). Grounded, though the Kuala Lumpur row labelled 'negative' is a stretch since staff aren't discussed."
    },
    {
      "name": "food_quality_judgment",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "rationale": "Somewhat redundant with the Food & Beverages column, but the text-derived sentiment adds interpretive value and NULLs are correctly assigned when food is unmentioned. Sample readings are reasonable (syrupy juice but fine meal→mixed). Grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "disruption_handling_quality",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "rationale": "Captures how well operational disruptions were managed — a distinct, actionable axis not in the original columns. Samples align (smooth flight→no_disruption, seat reallocation/rescheduling→poorly_handled). Well grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "compensation_resolution_outcome",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.8,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "rationale": "Resolution outcome is a valuable analytical lever for identifying unresolved grievances. Not_applicable dominates but is correct for non-complaint rows. Samples are consistent (seat dispute→unresolved_or_refused). Grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "value_for_money_perception",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "rationale": "Largely a restatement of the existing 'Value For Money' rating column, offering little new analytical leverage. Sample values are reasonable readings, so groundedness holds, but usefulness is limited by redundancy."
    },
    {
      "name": "service_consistency_concern",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.5,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "rationale": "A bare boolean flag that is somewhat generic and skewed (597 true). The concept is defensible, but the true/false assignment appears loosely warranted — e.g. the Kuala Lumpur schedule-change row marked True for consistency concern is thinly supported. Moderate on both dimensions."
    },
    {
      "name": "repeat_or_loyalty_signal",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.65,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "rationale": "Loyalty/repeat-customer signal is a genuinely useful new axis for retention analysis. Samples correctly default to False when no loyalty language appears. Grounded."
    },
    {
      "name": "special_needs_handling",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.55,
      "source_groundedness": 0.8,
      "rationale": "Very sparse (34 true) and near-constant, limiting analytical leverage, but it isolates a specific accessibility concern not otherwise captured. False defaults for silent rows are correct. Grounded."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
  "source_groundedness": 0.82,
  "rationale": "The added columns provide multiple genuinely useful axes (complaint category, disruption handling, resolution outcome, loyalty signal) that directly help an analyst decide what to concentrate on. A couple are weaker: value_for_money_perception largely restates an existing rating, and service_consistency_concern is a somewhat generic, loosely-warranted boolean. Groundedness is generally strong across samples, with correct NULL/false defaults for silent rows and sound categorisations; only minor over-confident labels (e.g. inferring negative staff attitude where staff are unmentioned) slightly reduce the score. No systematic hallucination observed."
}

